\begin{abstract}
Phase 1 found that, at a 05:00 decision time, tuned gradient boosting does not beat a regularised logistic regression on NYC 2013 flights. Phase 2 keeps the same label (arrival $\geq$15 min late), decision time, chronological split and single test evaluation, and asks whether deep learning reads the same information better. We build a compact MLP with learned embeddings for carrier, origin and destination; a temporal model in which a GRU reads the last <<d6['seq_len']>> days of delay outcomes, ordered by calendar day and ending on D$-$1; and a hybrid in which a temporal network learns only a correction on top of logistic regression's logit. Every network is tuned with the same leak-free rolling protocol as Phase 1, with epochs in place of trees. On November--December (base rate <<f3(d10['test_base_rate'])>>) the MLP scores PR AUC <<f3(T[MLP]['pr_auc'])>>, the temporal model <<f3(T[GRU]['pr_auc'])>> and the hybrid <<f3(T[HYB]['pr_auc'])>>, against <<f3(T[LR]['pr_auc'])>> for logistic regression and <<f3(T[XGB]['pr_auc'])>> for Phase 1's XGBoost. <<sig_sentence>> (paired day-block bootstrap); the gains are small. The temporal model needs none of the 22 hand-made history features, and removing its GRU costs <<pm(abl_rows[0]['change vs full'])>>. A classical ARIMA model of the daily late rate adds nothing when ranking flights. In an offline what-if, padding the hybrid's flags by <<d10['buffer_min']>> minutes avoids <<f"{pol['hybrid flags']['late arrivals avoided']:.0f}">> late arrivals against <<f"{pol['random flights']['late arrivals avoided']:.0f}">> for random flights with the same buffer budget.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
flight delay prediction, entity embeddings, GRU, hybrid model, temperature scaling, ablation, ARIMA, what-if analysis
