\subsection{Ordered Delay Histories}
The brief allows a temporal model only if history is correctly ordered. For a flight on day $D$ we build a sequence over days $t = D-<<d6['seq_len']>>, \dots, D-1$ with <<d6['n_channels']>> channels: the overall NYC late rate $p_t$ and log volume, and for six groups $g$ (origin, carrier, carrier at origin, route, flight number, aircraft) a smoothed rate \emph{relative} to that day's overall rate and the group's log volume,
\begin{equation}
\tilde r_{g,t} = \frac{k_{g,t} + \alpha\, p_t}{n_{g,t} + \alpha} - p_t,\qquad \alpha = <<d6['seq_alpha']>>,
\end{equation}
plus the weekday of $t$ (sin/cos). $k_{g,t}$ and $n_{g,t}$ are the late and labelled flights of group $g$ on day $t$. Days before the data start are zero. The training sequences take <<d6['train_seq_mb']>> MB (float32). Fig.~\ref{fig:seq} shows one flight's two weeks.

\textbf{Leakage test.} A unit test flips every label from a cut-off day onward: the sequences for that day must be identical, and to show the test can fail, the next day's must change. It passes, as do the two Phase 1 leakage tests.

