\subsection{Why Relative Rates: Drift on the Inputs}
Phase 1 blamed tree splits on absolute levels for boosting's autumn drop. We measure it on the inputs: a rule written in July, ``origin's 7-day rate in July's top quarter'', is applied to every month (Table~\ref{tab:drift}). On the absolute rate it fires on <<f2(min(r[ABS] for r in drift))>>--<<f2(max(r[ABS] for r in drift))>> of flights and on none in September--November; on the relative rate it fires on <<f2(min(r[REL] for r in drift))>>--<<f2(max(r[REL] for r in drift))>> in every month. A relative rate asks ``worse than everyone else right now?'', which means the same in every season.

