\section{Threats to Validity}
(i) One year of data cannot separate season from trend, and the test months (with the holiday peak) behave differently from July--September: the rolling months favoured only the hybrid, while the test months favour all three networks. We report both. (ii) The rolling months were used for every selection (MLP search, model choice, ablation), so rolling means are slightly optimistic; the test set is unaffected. (iii) Seeds move rolling means by up to <<f4(max(r['range'] for r in seeds.values()))>>, the same size as several of the differences we discuss. (iv) Temperature fitted on October helped some models and hurt others under the base-rate shift. (v) Our XGBoost refit differs from the Phase 1 report in the third decimal. (vi) The what-if ignores knock-on effects through the aircraft rotation and crews, prices every buffer minute equally, and only pads arrivals.

\section{Conclusion}
At a 05:00 decision time, deep learning reads the same information slightly better than logistic regression: on the test months the MLP (<<f3(T[MLP]['pr_auc'])>>), the temporal model (<<f3(T[GRU]['pr_auc'])>>) and the hybrid (<<f3(T[HYB]['pr_auc'])>>) all lie above logistic regression (<<f3(T[LR]['pr_auc'])>>) and Phase 1's XGBoost (<<f3(T[XGB]['pr_auc'])>>), but the gains are small, about two hundredths of PR AUC. The most interesting finding is that a GRU over raw, ordered daily outcomes replaces all 22 hand-made history features, and that the GRU is the one component whose removal clearly hurts. The model we would deploy is <<NICK[DEPLOY]>>: it was the only network ahead of logistic regression in every seed on the rolling months, it is significantly above it on the test months (<<dci(T[HYB])>>), and by construction it falls back to logistic regression. <<cap(NICK[best_test])>> scored highest on the test months, but choosing it now would be choosing on the test set. Future work: weather forecasts known at 05:00, several years of data, and an aircraft-rotation model so the what-if can include knock-on effects.

